      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Clinical studies have suggested that patients who take morphine for pain relief do not show a high degree of dependence. The present study examined the development of naloxone-precipitated withdrawal in rats receiving morphine in the presence or absence of formalin-induced pain. Morphine (10 mg/kg, i.p.) or saline was administered for 4 consecutive days 10 min after a subcutaneous injection of 50 microliters of 2.5% formalin or saline into the hind-paw. On the 5th day, rats were injected with naloxone (1 mg/kg, i.p.) and observed for signs of precipitated withdrawal (ptosis, teeth chattering and excretion/diarrhea). Naloxone-precipitated withdrawal symptoms were significantly greater in rats that received morphine in the absence of pain than in rats that received morphine in the presence of pain."},
